# How to Alias the Name of a Column in Eloquent

When you execute a query on the `products` table joined with a `tags` table, Laravel's Eloquent ORM automatically maps database columns to model attributes based on their casing and naming conventions. By default, a column named `name` in the `tags` table will appear as `name` in your resulting array. If you need that attribute to be accessible as `tag_name` instead, you must explicitly alias the column within your SQL query.

Laravel's relationship magic handles fetching related data, but it does not automatically rename columns during a raw join operation unless you instruct the query builder to do so. To achieve the desired output where `$result[$i]['tag_name']` exists, you need to use the `DB::raw()` function or the specific column aliasing syntax provided by the query builder.

The most direct approach is to modify your `scopeJoinWithTags` method to include an alias in the join condition or, more commonly, in a subsequent `select` statement. However, simply adding an alias during the `leftJoin` itself often only affects how the table is referenced in joins, not necessarily the final column names returned in the result set unless you are careful.

To ensure the attribute appears as `tag_name` in the final array, you should explicitly select the aliased column. Here is how you can implement this using the query builder syntax:

```php
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\DB;

public function scopeJoinWithTags($query)
{
    return $query->leftJoin('tags', 'tags.id', '=', 'products.tag_id')
        ->select(
            'products.*',
            DB::raw('tags.name as tag_name')
        );
}
```

In this snippet, the `DB::raw` function wraps the SQL expression `tags.name as tag_name`. This tells the database engine to return the column under the new name. When Eloquent hydrates the model or converts the collection to an array using `toArray()`, it reads these alias names directly from the result set. Consequently, accessing `$model->tag_name` or iterating over the array with `['tag_name']` becomes possible without needing to manually map keys afterwards.

If you prefer not to use the raw SQL function, you can utilize the query builder's string interpolation which is also supported in recent Laravel versions:

```php
use Illuminate\Database\Query\Builder;

public function scopeJoinWithTags($query)
{
    return $query->leftJoin('tags', 'tags.id', '=', 'products.tag_id')
        ->select(['products.*', 'tags.name as tag_name']);
}
```

This approach is cleaner and avoids the `DB::raw` helper, making the intent more obvious to other developers reading the code. It effectively solves the immediate problem of changing `$result[$i]['name']` to `$result[$i]['tag_name']`.

A critical consideration when aliasing columns in Eloquent involves model relationships. If your `Product` model has a `belongsToMany` or `belongsTo` relationship defined with the `tags` table, Eloquent might attempt to merge attributes from the related models into the main model's attribute array. If the column is aliased as `tag_name`, but the relationship expects a standard `name` attribute for filling, you may encounter discrepancies depending on how you access the data.

For instance, if you are using `toArray()` without specifying `['only' => ...]`, Eloquent will expose all columns found in the result set. If an alias exists, it takes precedence over the original column name in the array representation. However, if you rely on `$product->tags->name` to access data from the joined table via a relationship accessor, Eloquent looks for `name` specifically. In such cases, simply aliasing the raw join might not be enough; you may need to adjust the relationship mapping or use `with()` to eager load the relationship separately, ensuring the model knows how to map the aliased column back to its property.

It is also worth noting that aliases are specific to the current query execution. If you later modify the scope to remove the alias, all dependent code expecting `tag_name` will break. Always verify that any downstream logic relying on attribute names accounts for these changes, especially when sharing models across different parts of an application.
